# ── tsbench-forge → Hippius scrape host ──
# This host ONLY scrapes and pushes raw data to the Hippius bucket. The cascade
# eval-pool publish (cascade-pool publish) runs on the subnet-owner's cascade
# infra, pulling FROM this bucket — see publish-pool.yml in the cascade repo.
# Times are UTC. flock -n skips a run if the previous one is still going.
MAILTO=""

# Poll bands. Each runs ONE cron.yaml group whose cadence is finer than the
# 15-minute sweep; anything slower is left to the sweep, which is already faster
# than it needs. Id lists come from cron.yaml, so they track the catalog.
#
# Bands are the ONLY way to poll faster than the sweep: scraper.py --all gates on
# is_due(), which returns True for everything hourly-or-faster, so a PT5M source
# and a PT1M source in the sweep are both polled every 15 minutes.
#
# Fast band -- feeds returning a single "now" instant, where a missed minute is
# unrecoverable. 78 sources, ~15s a pass.
* * * * * /usr/bin/flock -n /tmp/tsforge_scrape_fast.lock /root/cron/scrape_band.sh '* * * * *' fast 4

# 5-minute band. cron.yaml declared this group for a long time and nothing ran
# it, so its members were declared at five minutes and delivered at fifteen: of
# 93 GBFS feeds declaring PT5M, exactly one was observing PT5M.
#
# 48 sources, ~9s a pass. It was 159 for one afternoon and that was wrong twice
# over: 111 of them are PUBLISHER-limited (their API returns timestamped history,
# so the 15-min sweep already backfills every point and a faster poll buys
# nothing), and the resulting 33s pass contended with the every-minute band hard
# enough to push it past its own tick -- gaps of 93-144s against 60s, on exactly
# the feeds whose missed minutes cannot be recovered. Membership is now measured:
# a source belongs here only if its OBSERVED cadence equals our poll rate, i.e.
# we are the bottleneck rather than the publisher.
# NOT SCHEDULED. Measured 2026-08-16: enabling it, even narrowed to 48 sources
# and 9s of work, pushed the every-minute band from a 20% tick-drop rate to 80%.
# The every-minute band does 46-52s of fetching plus a ~18s interpreter import
# against a 60s tick, so it sits ON the cliff edge -- baseline median gap 62s,
# almost exactly one tick -- and any extra load on these two cores tips it from
# "just makes it" to "drops every other one". Those feeds exist precisely
# because a missed minute cannot be recovered, so they outrank a 3x gain on 48.
#
# To afford this band, first buy headroom in the minute tick. The obvious target
# is the ~18s import paid on EVERY invocation -- 30% of the tick spent importing
# pyarrow and httpx -- which a resident worker would pay once.
# */5 * * * * /usr/bin/flock -n /tmp/tsforge_scrape_5min.lock /root/cron/scrape_band.sh '*/5 * * * *' 5min 3

# Full sweep every 15 min: scrape every source (rolling-window feeds backfill via
# dedup). Runs ~709s. Scrape only — the upload is a separate job below, because
# sharing this lock with it put the pair at 17.4 min against a 15-minute tick and
# flock -n silently dropped every other run, halving the catalog's sample rate.
*/15 * * * * /usr/bin/flock -n /tmp/tsforge_scrape_all.lock /root/cron/scrape_all.sh

# Mirror data/ + sources.yaml to Hippius S3, on its own lock so a slow upload
# can never delay a scrape. Offset ~1 min past each sweep's finish. Safe to
# overlap a sweep: parquet writes are atomic and the sync is size-diff
# idempotent, so a file caught mid-growth just re-sends next pass.
13,28,43,58 * * * * /usr/bin/flock -n /tmp/tsforge_sync.lock /root/cron/sync_hippius.sh

# Daily freshness audit (report-only): newest observation vs declared cadence.
20 6 * * * /usr/bin/flock -n /tmp/tsforge_audit.lock /root/cron/audit_daily.sh

# Hourly log rotation: the scrape logs are append-only and unbounded (they had
# reached 315MB combined); a full disk would look like a total upstream outage.
7 * * * * /usr/bin/flock -n /tmp/tsforge_rotate.lock /root/cron/rotate_logs.sh

# Daily eval-pool composition (report-only): eligible sources/series per domain,
# the domain x cadence grid, the backlog still short of min_series_length, and a
# fixed-seed sample draw. Runs after the audit.
#
# NB the old comment here claimed this took the same lock as scrape_all "so the
# two heavy parquet readers never overlap on this 4GB box" — it never did, it
# has always had its own lock. What actually kept them apart was the clock: at
# :40 it sat in the gap between sweeps ending :27 and starting :45. Splitting
# the upload out (2026-08-16) moved sweeps to :00/:15/:30/:45, which would have
# put a sweep across :30-:42 straight through this job's start, so it moves to
# :43 to sit in the new gap. This box has an OOM history (8 kills, last
# 2026-08-09 at 2.85GB) and a sweep peaks near 700MB — keep them off each other.
43 6 * * * /usr/bin/flock -n /tmp/tsforge_pool.lock /root/cron/pool_report.sh

# Daily source grind: find new sources steered at the thinnest domains, wire
# the survivors, commit (never pushes). Target 5, floor 2 — under the floor
# means the veins stopped producing rather than a quiet day. Refuses to run on
# a dirty catalog or off GRIND_BRANCH, and stages only sources.yaml+cron.yaml.
# Moved :10 -> :13 with pool_report above, for the same reason: sweeps now run
# :00-:12 and this used to start in the clear.
13 7 * * * /usr/bin/flock -n /tmp/tsforge_grind.lock /root/cron/grind_daily.sh
